### Description

On Linux ARM64 with .NET SDK 10.0.402 / runtime 10.0.13 (official sdk:10.0-noble image), publishing a source-generated System.Text.Json console app with trimming succeeds after an API usage edit, but the resulting executable fails to resolve System.IO.Pipelines. The second publish reuses obj/bin and the publish output. No third-party library, AutoSDK, reflection serialization or NuGet PackageReference is required.

### Reproduction

Create Probe.csproj:
```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk"><PropertyGroup><TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework><OutputType>Exe</OutputType><ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings><Nullable>enable</Nullable><TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors><PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed><TrimMode>full</TrimMode><JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>false</JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault></PropertyGroup></Project>
```
Program.cs, first version:
```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
var model = JsonSerializer.Deserialize("{\"Value\":\"works\"}", OrdinaryContext.Default.UsedModel);
Console.WriteLine("stock-passed"); return 0;
public sealed class UsedModel { public string? Value { get; set; } }
[System.Text.Json.Serialization.JsonSerializable(typeof(UsedModel))]
internal sealed partial class OrdinaryContext : System.Text.Json.Serialization.JsonSerializerContext { }

```
Run `dotnet publish Probe.csproj -c Release -r linux-arm64 --self-contained true -o publish` and `./publish/Probe`: succeeds.

Replace only Program.cs with:
```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
var model = JsonSerializer.Deserialize("{\"Value\":\"works\"}", OrdinaryContext.Default.UsedModel);
if (!JsonSerializer.Serialize(model, OrdinaryContext.Default.UsedModel).Contains("works")) return 2;
Console.WriteLine("stock-passed"); return 0;
public sealed class UsedModel { public string? Value { get; set; } }
[System.Text.Json.Serialization.JsonSerializable(typeof(UsedModel))]
internal sealed partial class OrdinaryContext : System.Text.Json.Serialization.JsonSerializerContext { }

```
Repeat the same publish and executable commands without deleting any outputs: publish succeeds, execution fails with FileNotFoundException for System.IO.Pipelines, Version=10.0.0.0 from JsonSerializer.WriteString<TValue>.

### Observed incremental output

Both published .deps.json files were byte-identical (SHA256 9d21d960065d17e150acbf6317539b68c87dd431de66dd636ead4cf83c0f4cec), omitting System.IO.Pipelines. The build deps file under bin includes it, whereas the intermediate trimmed deps file under obj and published copy omit it. The framework assembly is present in the second publish output. A fresh artifacts directory passes. Deleting only obj/Release/net10.0/linux-arm64/Probe.deps.json and repeating the second publish also passes while retaining NuGet/compiler/linker caches.

### Suspected missing input

ILLink recomputes _RemovedManagedAssembly and removes those items from RuntimePackAsset / runtime copy items. GeneratePublishDependencyFile is incremental, but _GeneratePublishDependencyFileInputCache hashes static properties and not the resulting trimmed runtime assembly set. A change in reachable APIs can therefore alter the runtime graph without invalidating the dependency-file target. Please include the resulting runtime graph (or appropriate ILLink result stamp) in that target's incremental inputs while retaining no-op cache behavior.

Local tracking: https://github.com/tryAGI/AutoSDK/issues/432. This issue reproduces independently of that generator.
